1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,258
Start the application with Run RepoChronos dot bat.

2
00:00:04,258 --> 00:00:07,131
Open the local address in your browser.

3
00:00:07,131 --> 00:00:10,741
Sign in with your own account, or register first.

4
00:00:10,741 --> 00:00:13,615
Here, Alex Student is a fictional user.

5
00:00:13,615 --> 00:00:21,351
All records in this video are illustrative sample values rendered using the actual application templates.

6
00:00:22,958 --> 00:00:26,973
On the repository page, expand Connect a repository.

7
00:00:26,973 --> 00:00:33,072
Enter a real GitHub URL, or an owner slash repository name, then click Connect.

8
00:00:33,072 --> 00:00:36,160
Course Library is our fictional example.

9
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:40,483
A successful connection gives your account owner access.

10
00:00:40,483 --> 00:00:46,659
The live demonstration requires internet access and sufficient GitHub API quota.

11
00:00:48,292 --> 00:00:51,564
Open the repository and click Sync latest.

12
00:00:51,564 --> 00:00:55,304
Use Continue import if more pages are available.

13
00:00:55,304 --> 00:01:03,564
Change history shows which developer changed each file, the commit, timestamp, and added or deleted lines.

14
00:01:03,564 --> 00:01:08,161
In this example, Alex and Maya both changed library dot py.

15
00:01:08,161 --> 00:01:10,343
Filter by file or developer.

16
00:01:10,343 --> 00:01:13,927
Reimporting the same commit avoids duplicates.

17
00:01:15,542 --> 00:01:19,987
Open Complexity and click Analyze next fifteen revisions.

18
00:01:19,987 --> 00:01:21,313
Repeat as needed.

19
00:01:21,313 --> 00:01:27,318
Lizard analyzes supported languages and stores every detected function score.

20
00:01:27,318 --> 00:01:32,621
Here, a Python function has scores of four and eight in two commits.

21
00:01:32,621 --> 00:01:36,209
With threshold five, eight receives a warning.

22
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:38,626
Book dot java is also analyzed.

23
00:01:38,626 --> 00:01:41,200
README is explicitly unsupported.

24
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:47,361
Complexity indicates maintainability risk; it does not prove a software defect.

25
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,167
In Compare revisions, choose a file and two commits that both changed it.

26
00:01:54,167 --> 00:01:59,759
Analyze both revisions using the same Lizard version first, then click Compare.

27
00:01:59,759 --> 00:02:05,493
In our sample, calculate fine increases from four to eight, a difference of four.

28
00:02:05,493 --> 00:02:08,678
The comparison reads historical measurements.

29
00:02:08,678 --> 00:02:12,925
Earlier scores stay preserved when new commits are analyzed.

30
00:02:14,542 --> 00:02:17,566
Open Bug tracker and expand Report a bug.

31
00:02:17,566 --> 00:02:20,737
Enter a clear title and reproduction steps.

32
00:02:20,737 --> 00:02:26,269
Our fictional report describes a negative fine when returning a book early.

33
00:02:26,269 --> 00:02:30,179
Explain what you expected and what actually happened.

34
00:02:30,179 --> 00:02:31,506
Submit the report.

35
00:02:31,506 --> 00:02:35,120
The application records who reported it and when.

36
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:38,587
A complexity warning alone is not a bug report.

37
00:02:40,208 --> 00:02:45,175
After reporting, select a repository contributor and click Assign.

38
00:02:45,175 --> 00:02:49,539
In this example, Maya is assigned and work is in progress.

39
00:02:49,539 --> 00:02:52,098
Mark in progress when work begins.

40
00:02:52,098 --> 00:02:58,570
Once the actual fix has been pushed to GitHub, click Sync latest to import its commit.

41
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:02,934
Select that fixing commit, then click Resolve with commit.

42
00:03:04,542 --> 00:03:06,390
The issue now shows Fixed.

43
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:12,717
Expand Event history to review assignment, progress, acting users, and the fixing commit.

44
00:03:12,717 --> 00:03:20,751
Our fictional report was opened at ten thirty and resolved at twelve thirty, so the resolution time is two hours.

45
00:03:20,751 --> 00:03:23,239
These timestamps are sample values.

46
00:03:23,239 --> 00:03:28,002
The system records the sequence without overwriting earlier events.

47
00:03:29,625 --> 00:03:31,116
Return to Overview.

48
00:03:31,116 --> 00:03:37,863
Our sample now shows three commits, three files, zero open bugs, and one resolved bug.

49
00:03:37,863 --> 00:03:40,766
Alex and Maya appear as contributors.

50
00:03:40,766 --> 00:03:44,846
Complexity rises and then falls in the stored trend.

51
00:03:44,846 --> 00:03:50,259
The earlier warning remains part of history even after a simpler fix.

52
00:03:50,259 --> 00:03:55,280
Dashboard values in the live project come from Supabase records.

53
00:03:56,917 --> 00:04:02,017
In SQL workbench, show files changed by more than one developer.

54
00:04:02,017 --> 00:04:08,791
This query groups file history by path and uses HAVING to return collaborative files.

55
00:04:08,791 --> 00:04:12,457
Library dot py has two authors in our example.

56
00:04:12,457 --> 00:04:19,071
In Supabase, run the examiner SQL file to explain joins, keys, views, and triggers.

57
00:04:19,071 --> 00:04:24,809
RepoChronos uses Laravel, Blade, Supabase PostgreSQL, and Python Lizard.

58
00:04:24,809 --> 00:04:30,786
This illustrated demonstration inserted no records into your live database.
